From: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
Subject: [PATCH] input: joystick: mcu-joystick: power the MCU off in suspend

The AYANEO SPI MCU behind the built-in gamepad stays powered for the whole
system sleep: nothing deasserts power(125)/en(92), and the 1.2 V logic rail
is on-in-suspend because it is shared with the codec's vdd-px. The MCU keeps
running its ~250 Hz frame loop (and whatever else its board carries) for the
entire sleep window, and on the KONKR Pocket FIT Elite that is current that
buys nothing — the pad cannot wake the system, its IRQ is masked at suspend
and it is not a wakeup source.

Drop the load switch and the enable pin at system suspend, and put the MCU
through the vendor power-on sequence on resume. This is the same sequence
mcu_power_dance() already runs to recover a wedged MCU, so it is the power
cycle the vendor driver uses itself.

Two things are deliberately not touched:
- vdd (vreg_l2i_1p2): shared with the WCD939x vdd-px rail, cannot be cut here.
- The UART MCU (konkr_sysbtn): its board has no modelled supply, so there is
  nothing to cut from this driver. It already blanks the stick LEDs before
  suspend and resends its enable frame afterwards, so it survives the SPI MCU
  losing power if the two interfaces share one board.

mcu_joystick.power_off_in_suspend=0 restores the old behaviour, for A/B
measurement on hardware.
--- a/drivers/input/joystick/mcu_joystick.c
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/mcu_joystick.c
@@ -606,7 +606,22 @@
  * SPI controller ("Attempted to sync while suspend"), thrashing init commands
  * and full power cycles against a dead bus for the whole suspend window, and
  * the gamepad only comes back if the watchdog happens to win afterwards.
+ *
+ * On top of that the MCU board keeps drawing current for the whole sleep, so
+ * suspend also drops its power. The 1.2 V logic rail (vdd) cannot be used for
+ * that: it is shared with the codec's vdd-px and is on-in-suspend in LPM. The
+ * load switch (power) and the enable pin (en) are what actually remove power,
+ * and deasserting them in that order is the sequence mcu_power_dance() already
+ * uses to recover a wedged MCU, i.e. the vendor's own power cycle.
+ *
+ * mcu_joystick.power_off_in_suspend=0, or writing 0 to the matching sysfs
+ * parameter, restores the old behaviour for A/B measurement.
  */
+static bool power_off_in_suspend = true;
+module_param(power_off_in_suspend, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(power_off_in_suspend,
+		 "Cut the MCU's power for system sleep (default: on)");
+
 static int mcu_joystick_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct mcu_joystick *mcu = spi_get_drvdata(to_spi_device(dev));
@@ -620,6 +635,13 @@
 	mcu_write_word(mcu, MCU_WRITE_WORD(MCU_REG_RUMBLE_STRONG, 0));
 	mcu_write_word(mcu, MCU_WRITE_WORD(MCU_REG_RUMBLE_WEAK, 0));
 	mcu_report_idle(mcu);
+
+	if (power_off_in_suspend) {
+		/* en first, then the load switch — the vendor's own order */
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(mcu->en_gpio, 0);
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(mcu->power_gpio, 0);
+		dev_info(dev, "MCU power cut for suspend\n");
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -627,19 +649,25 @@
 static int mcu_joystick_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct mcu_joystick *mcu = spi_get_drvdata(to_spi_device(dev));
-	u16 regs[MCU_NREGS];
 
 	/*
-	 * Vendor resume recipe: re-send the irq-rate + start commands. If the
-	 * MCU lost power across the suspend it answers zeros — give it the
-	 * full power-on sequence then.
+	 * suspend() cut the MCU's power, so it cannot answer the cheap
+	 * init-command probe: run the vendor power-on sequence first. With the
+	 * power cut disabled, keep the old probe-then-cycle recipe.
 	 */
-	mcu_send_init_cmds(mcu);
-	if (mcu_read_frame(mcu, regs) || !mcu_frame_hdr_ok(regs)) {
-		dev_info(dev, "resume: MCU silent after init cmds, power cycling\n");
+	if (power_off_in_suspend) {
+		dev_info(dev, "resume: power cycling the MCU\n");
 		mcu_power_dance(mcu);
+	} else {
+		u16 regs[MCU_NREGS];
+
 		mcu_send_init_cmds(mcu);
+		if (mcu_read_frame(mcu, regs) || !mcu_frame_hdr_ok(regs)) {
+			dev_info(dev, "resume: MCU silent after init cmds, power cycling\n");
+			mcu_power_dance(mcu);
+		}
 	}
+	mcu_send_init_cmds(mcu);
 
 	mcu->bad_frames = 0;
 	mcu->reinit_fails = 0;
